WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.040
Welcome to Module 2 of
the Data Platform and SQL

2
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:06.400
Server Identity learning path:
the authentication audit.

3
00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:08.108
It teaches Runbook R oh three,

4
00:00:08.108 --> 00:00:12.650
with Chapter 2 of the developer
workbook, Building SQL Server Identity.

5
00:00:12.650 --> 00:00:15.438
Module 1 decided which instances survive.

6
00:00:15.438 --> 00:00:18.420
Now you measure how each
survivor authenticates.

7
00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:23.557
Why does this matter?

8
00:00:23.557 --> 00:00:28.507
For each instance that survived the gate,
the audit records its authentication mode,

9
00:00:28.507 --> 00:00:31.507
every usable login by type,
the state of the S A login,

10
00:00:31.507 --> 00:00:33.507
and the account the engine runs as.

11
00:00:33.507 --> 00:00:35.066
Every later step, Arc,

12
00:00:35.066 --> 00:00:39.744
login migration and N T L M
elimination, depends on that record.

13
00:00:39.744 --> 00:00:43.008
And it's re-run after every
change, to measure progress.

14
00:00:43.008 --> 00:00:45.941
In this module you audit
four fictional instances,

15
00:00:45.941 --> 00:00:49.294
rank their findings, and rerun
the audit thirty days later.

16
00:00:49.294 --> 00:00:52.920
Runbook R oh three is
read-only from start to finish.

17
00:00:56.720 --> 00:00:59.436
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

18
00:00:59.436 --> 00:01:01.720
which takes about fifty
minutes with the lab.

19
00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:04.783
You'll explain what the
audit reads on each instance,

20
00:01:04.783 --> 00:01:08.620
and why it runs only on what Module
1 retained or chose as a target.

21
00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:10.353
You'll rank the audit's findings,

22
00:01:10.353 --> 00:01:13.420
from an enabled S A down to
the Arc-eligibility category.

23
00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:17.276
You'll say what each Arc-eligibility
category means for the next step,

24
00:01:17.276 --> 00:01:21.470
and why engine Entra authentication
doesn't wait for O S level Entra sign-in.

25
00:01:21.470 --> 00:01:24.485
And you'll explain why a single
audit run can't show drift,

26
00:01:24.485 --> 00:01:26.845
and where the engine's
service account goes.

27
00:01:26.845 --> 00:01:29.540
Before you start, finish Module 1.

28
00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:35.452
Unit 2 is what the audit reads.

29
00:01:35.452 --> 00:01:39.916
It runs only on instances classified
retain or consolidation target.

30
00:01:39.916 --> 00:01:42.649
For each, it reads the
authentication mode,

31
00:01:42.649 --> 00:01:47.882
and enumerates the logins from the server
principals view by type: Windows users,

32
00:01:47.882 --> 00:01:53.193
Windows groups, SQL logins, logins mapped
to a certificate or an asymmetric key,

33
00:01:53.193 --> 00:01:57.177
and the Entra types, E for a user
or application and X for a group.

34
00:01:57.177 --> 00:02:02.266
It flags the S A account, and captures the
engine's service account and its S P N.

35
00:02:02.266 --> 00:02:06.569
The running mode and the registry login
mode are read in different places,

36
00:02:06.569 --> 00:02:10.590
and a change takes effect only after
a restart, so the two can disagree.

37
00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:13.003
The kit reports that as a mode anomaly.

38
00:02:13.003 --> 00:02:15.694
And watch for this: a
reader without the right

39
00:02:15.694 --> 00:02:18.603
permissions gets a short
login list and no error.

40
00:02:18.603 --> 00:02:22.189
A short list means a missing
permission, not a clean instance.

41
00:02:22.189 --> 00:02:27.203
The kit's census leaves out built-in
principals, and counts only usable logins.

42
00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:33.185
The audit returns one row per finding,

43
00:02:33.185 --> 00:02:37.838
with a severity from the data platform
book's chapter, The Authentication Audit.

44
00:02:37.838 --> 00:02:41.259
Highest is S A enabled:
the login whose security

45
00:02:41.259 --> 00:02:45.176
identifier is oh x oh one is
enabled, whatever its name.

46
00:02:45.176 --> 00:02:47.850
Critical is any other usable SQL login.

47
00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:51.403
High is mixed mode, where SQL
authentication is accepted,

48
00:02:51.403 --> 00:02:55.525
and a mode anomaly, where the registry
disagrees with the running mode.

49
00:02:55.525 --> 00:02:59.949
Medium is the engine running as a domain
user account or a built-in account,

50
00:02:59.949 --> 00:03:02.488
and an S A that's disabled
but keeps its name.

51
00:03:02.488 --> 00:03:07.250
And at the bottom, an information row for
each instance's Arc-eligibility category.

52
00:03:07.250 --> 00:03:11.063
The kit's audit script writes
the census and the findings,

53
00:03:11.063 --> 00:03:15.525
each as a C S V and a jay-son file,
and one manifest that hashes all four.

54
00:03:15.525 --> 00:03:18.179
A disabled login still counts as existing:

55
00:03:18.179 --> 00:03:21.530
Chapter 9's closure record
decides when it's gone.

56
00:03:25.330 --> 00:03:27.468
Unit 3 is reading the audit.

57
00:03:27.468 --> 00:03:31.318
Its most consequential output is
the Arc-eligibility category.

58
00:03:31.318 --> 00:03:34.043
Category 1 is ready for Entra logins now.

59
00:03:34.043 --> 00:03:38.642
Category 2 needs Arc deployed and
Entra authentication configured first.

60
00:03:38.642 --> 00:03:41.867
Within it, instances with
many Windows logins,

61
00:03:41.867 --> 00:03:45.092
active SQL logins or
missing S P Ns go first.

62
00:03:45.092 --> 00:03:50.654
Category 3 is a version before SQL Server
2022, or a failover cluster instance

63
00:03:50.654 --> 00:03:55.468
on any version, and must upgrade,
re-platform or carry a dated exception.

64
00:03:55.468 --> 00:04:00.195
Engine Entra authentication, Layer
1, proceeds on a domain-joined host,

65
00:04:00.195 --> 00:04:03.455
and never waits for O S
level Entra sign-in, Layer 2.

66
00:04:03.455 --> 00:04:06.755
The kit settles only the first pass here,

67
00:04:06.755 --> 00:04:10.355
Category 3 or "1 or 2";
Module 3 settles the rest.

68
00:04:10.355 --> 00:04:13.530
And the audit names the engine's
service account and stops.

69
00:04:13.530 --> 00:04:18.260
Converting a user account used as a service
belongs to the service account guide.

70
00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:24.122
What does each category ask of you?

71
00:04:24.122 --> 00:04:28.384
The data platform book's chapter,
Reading the Audit, takes them in turn.

72
00:04:28.384 --> 00:04:32.657
For a Category 1 instance, nothing in
the infrastructure remains to be built:

73
00:04:32.657 --> 00:04:36.522
the audit has done its job when it
delivers a precise login inventory.

74
00:04:36.522 --> 00:04:40.362
For Category 2, the Arc deployment
is a server-level action,

75
00:04:40.362 --> 00:04:42.122
not a SQL Server change.

76
00:04:42.122 --> 00:04:43.890
The agent connects the server,

77
00:04:43.890 --> 00:04:48.061
and Arc installs the Azure extension
for SQL Server automatically.

78
00:04:48.061 --> 00:04:51.413
Once Entra authentication is
configured on the instance,

79
00:04:51.413 --> 00:04:53.998
the next audit re-classifies
it to Category 1.

80
00:04:53.998 --> 00:05:00.033
A Category 3 instance has two paths: upgrade
the engine to SQL Server 2022 or later

81
00:05:00.033 --> 00:05:05.723
and follow the Category 2 path, or document
a formal exception with a sunset date.

82
00:05:05.723 --> 00:05:08.407
A failover cluster
instance re-platforms to a

83
00:05:08.407 --> 00:05:11.161
standalone instance or
an availability group.

84
00:05:11.161 --> 00:05:16.397
And mind the program's N T
L M backstop, April 1, 2027.

85
00:05:16.397 --> 00:05:20.602
A Category 3 instance's remaining
N T L M connections fail then,

86
00:05:20.602 --> 00:05:22.498
whether it's excepted or not.

87
00:05:22.498 --> 00:05:24.800
Its Kerberos connections don't.

88
00:05:28.600 --> 00:05:32.369
Runbook R oh three's audit script
degrades rather than crashing,

89
00:05:32.369 --> 00:05:34.075
so know what each state means.

90
00:05:34.075 --> 00:05:38.132
If the Active Directory module is
absent, S P N discovery is skipped:

91
00:05:38.132 --> 00:05:42.537
only the hosts you name are audited,
and orphaned logins can't be checked.

92
00:05:42.537 --> 00:05:45.204
If the domain controller is unreachable,

93
00:05:45.204 --> 00:05:48.812
the S P N query returns nothing,
so target a reachable one.

94
00:05:48.812 --> 00:05:51.471
If a host can't be reached
for its service details,

95
00:05:51.471 --> 00:05:54.462
the service-account fields
stay unknown for that host.

96
00:05:54.462 --> 00:05:58.673
If the SQL connection fails, the
instance is recorded as query failed,

97
00:05:58.673 --> 00:06:02.112
with an empty login inventory,
rather than aborting the run.

98
00:06:02.112 --> 00:06:06.800
And a T C P timeout is recorded as
timeout, as opposed to yes or no.

99
00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:11.350
An empty or unknown field is a
state to read, not a clean result.

100
00:06:15.150 --> 00:06:16.037
Try it.

101
00:06:16.037 --> 00:06:19.508
Lab 1 runs in PowerShell 7 on
the kit's fictional estate,

102
00:06:19.508 --> 00:06:21.463
with no instance and no tenant.

103
00:06:21.463 --> 00:06:24.558
Go to the kit's folder, and
make this run's results folder,

104
00:06:24.558 --> 00:06:28.824
named for the time you start, so running
the lab again never overwrites a run.

105
00:06:28.824 --> 00:06:31.463
Step one reads the four audited instances.

106
00:06:31.463 --> 00:06:34.425
Only the instances Chapter
1 retained are audited.

107
00:06:34.425 --> 00:06:37.804
Look at sequel P R D oh
one: it runs in mixed mode,

108
00:06:37.804 --> 00:06:41.275
zero, while its registry
says Windows only, one.

109
00:06:41.275 --> 00:06:45.350
Sequel F C I oh four is clustered,
and its registry says mixed.

110
00:06:45.350 --> 00:06:48.592
Three of the four engines
run as domain user accounts,

111
00:06:48.592 --> 00:06:50.950
the S V C accounts in the contoso domain.

112
00:06:50.950 --> 00:06:53.971
And sequel P R D oh six,
on SQL Server 2025,

113
00:06:53.971 --> 00:06:59.370
runs as a group managed service account,
whose name ends in a dollar sign.

114
00:07:03.170 --> 00:07:04.908
Step two runs the audit.

115
00:07:04.908 --> 00:07:07.208
First the census: one row per instance,

116
00:07:07.208 --> 00:07:11.833
with usable logins counted by type,
and disabled ones counted separately.

117
00:07:11.833 --> 00:07:13.120
Then the findings.

118
00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:18.388
Sequel P R D oh one has the full set: S
A enabled, highest; a usable SQL login,

119
00:07:18.388 --> 00:07:22.964
payroll report, critical; mixed
mode and a mode anomaly, both high,

120
00:07:22.964 --> 00:07:26.332
because the mode was
changed without a restart;

121
00:07:26.332 --> 00:07:30.995
and a service account that's a user
account with a password, medium.

122
00:07:30.995 --> 00:07:34.877
The cut rows give sequel F C I
oh four almost the same list,

123
00:07:34.877 --> 00:07:38.083
with its own usable SQL
login, vendor H R app.

124
00:07:38.083 --> 00:07:42.044
That login has a dated exception,
but the audit reports it anyway;

125
00:07:42.044 --> 00:07:45.108
Chapter 9's closure record
applies the exception.

126
00:07:45.108 --> 00:07:49.920
Sequel F C I oh four is Category
3, as a failover cluster instance.

127
00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:54.508
Sequel P R D oh two has only its
service-account finding and its category.

128
00:07:54.508 --> 00:07:57.322
Sequel P R D oh six
already has Entra logins,

129
00:07:57.322 --> 00:08:02.308
runs as a group managed service account,
and has no finding but its category.

130
00:08:02.308 --> 00:08:08.195
The last line names the evidence:
A C two, I A five and C M six.

131
00:08:11.990 --> 00:08:15.060
Step three opens the manifest: four files,

132
00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:18.852
three controls and three K
S Is, with each file's hash.

133
00:08:18.852 --> 00:08:20.302
The run was read-only.

134
00:08:20.302 --> 00:08:25.638
Step four counts the findings by severity:
two highest, the two enabled S A logins;

135
00:08:25.638 --> 00:08:30.085
two critical, the two usable SQL
logins; three high; three medium,

136
00:08:30.085 --> 00:08:35.810
the three service accounts; and four
information rows, one category per instance.

137
00:08:39.610 --> 00:08:42.285
Step five reruns the audit on day 30.

138
00:08:42.285 --> 00:08:45.398
Sequel P R D oh one has
moved to Entra logins.

139
00:08:45.398 --> 00:08:48.689
Its S A, renamed, a
Windows login and payroll

140
00:08:48.689 --> 00:08:51.885
report are disabled, so disabled is three.

141
00:08:51.885 --> 00:08:55.827
Its two superseded Windows groups
were denied connect SQL instead,

142
00:08:55.827 --> 00:08:59.435
so they leave the usable count
without being counted as disabled.

143
00:08:59.435 --> 00:09:03.910
Nothing was dropped, and only its
service-account finding and category remain.

144
00:09:03.910 --> 00:09:06.485
Now look at sequel P R D oh six.

145
00:09:06.485 --> 00:09:10.623
Its Windows user count went from
zero to one, and no finding says so.

146
00:09:10.623 --> 00:09:14.961
A single audit doesn't compare
runs; Chapter 9's drift check does.

147
00:09:14.961 --> 00:09:18.997
Step six runs this chapter's
tests: six pass, none fail.

148
00:09:18.997 --> 00:09:20.797
Step seven lists your runs.

149
00:09:20.797 --> 00:09:24.710
There's nothing to clean up: the
results stay in the run's own folder.

150
00:09:28.510 --> 00:09:31.060
Lab 2 runs on your own test instance.

151
00:09:31.060 --> 00:09:33.035
It hasn't been run by the authors.

152
00:09:33.035 --> 00:09:37.173
You need a test SQL Server instance,
never production, PowerShell 7 with

153
00:09:37.173 --> 00:09:41.110
the SQL Server module, and read
permissions granted for the lab only.

154
00:09:41.110 --> 00:09:42.360
Never sysadmin.

155
00:09:42.360 --> 00:09:45.048
Check the reading login's
own permissions first.

156
00:09:45.048 --> 00:09:49.385
Then export a SELECT-only audit snapshot,
and compare its login count with one

157
00:09:49.385 --> 00:09:53.722
the instance's administrator trusts: a
short list means a missing permission.

158
00:09:53.722 --> 00:09:54.747
Then audit it.

159
00:09:54.747 --> 00:09:56.634
Nothing on the instance changes.

160
00:09:56.634 --> 00:09:58.765
To back it out, delete the audit run,

161
00:09:58.765 --> 00:10:01.960
and have the administrator
remove the lab's grants.

162
00:10:01.960 --> 00:10:05.430
Keep the snapshot if you
go on to Module 3's Lab 2.

163
00:10:09.230 --> 00:10:10.830
The federal requirement.

164
00:10:10.830 --> 00:10:16.680
Chapter 2's table maps the audit to
three controls in NIST SP 800-53.

165
00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:20.197
The login census supports A
C two, Account Management,

166
00:10:20.197 --> 00:10:23.555
with the census files and
the manifest as evidence.

167
00:10:23.555 --> 00:10:27.876
The S A, SQL login and service-account
findings support I A five,

168
00:10:27.876 --> 00:10:29.717
Authenticator Management.

169
00:10:29.717 --> 00:10:33.892
And the mode findings support C
M six, Configuration Settings.

170
00:10:33.892 --> 00:10:36.859
Runbook R oh three is
read-only from start to finish,

171
00:10:36.859 --> 00:10:39.967
and its output feeds
continuous-monitoring closure.

172
00:10:39.967 --> 00:10:44.240
K S Is are FedRAMP twenty X indicators
for cloud service providers:

173
00:10:44.240 --> 00:10:47.192
the agency lines its own
evidence up with them.

174
00:10:47.192 --> 00:10:50.207
The run's files provide
evidence for each control;

175
00:10:50.207 --> 00:10:54.330
the agency still sets its
parameters, and the assessor decides.

176
00:10:58.130 --> 00:11:00.180
Check yourself with two questions.

177
00:11:00.180 --> 00:11:03.018
First: which finding does
the audit rank highest?

178
00:11:03.018 --> 00:11:05.692
The answer is b: S A enabled.

179
00:11:05.692 --> 00:11:10.804
Second: on day 30, sequel P R D oh
six has gained a Windows login.

180
00:11:10.804 --> 00:11:13.205
What does the day-30 audit say about it?

181
00:11:13.205 --> 00:11:15.530
The answer is a: nothing.

182
00:11:15.530 --> 00:11:19.868
A single audit doesn't compare
runs; Chapter 9's drift check does.

183
00:11:19.868 --> 00:11:24.510
The module page has two more questions,
each linked to the section that answers it.

184
00:11:28.310 --> 00:11:29.335
To sum up.

185
00:11:29.335 --> 00:11:33.656
You learned what the audit reads on each
surviving instance, how its findings rank,

186
00:11:33.656 --> 00:11:36.998
and what each Arc-eligibility
category means for the next step.

187
00:11:36.998 --> 00:11:39.600
You audited four fictional instances,

188
00:11:39.600 --> 00:11:42.935
and saw why one audit run, on
its own, can't show drift.

189
00:11:42.935 --> 00:11:46.754
The evidence an I S S O keeps from
this module is the run folder,

190
00:11:46.754 --> 00:11:51.260
the census and findings files, and
the manifest holding each file's hash.

191
00:11:51.260 --> 00:11:54.185
Before shipping, work through
Chapter 2's checklist.

192
00:11:54.185 --> 00:11:57.635
Next is Module 3, Arc and
the engine's identity.
